I think this is worth making a first-class Pantheon artifact because it generalizes much of what we’ve been discussing while staying grounded. Rather than “persuasion,” the focus is on designing representations that reliably induce the intended reasoning process. That’s broader and applies to teaching, interfaces, documentation, games, programming languages, and collaboration.

Pantheon Artifact: Computational Steering

The highest-bandwidth communication is not transmitting conclusions. It is reliably inducing the intended computation.

Core Principle

Every message performs two jobs:

1. It transfers information.
2. It shapes the computation performed by the receiver.

The second is often more valuable.

Instead of optimizing for:

Sender \rightarrow Information \rightarrow Receiver

optimize for:

Sender
\rightarrow
Representation
\rightarrow
Receiver\ Computation
\rightarrow
Desired\ Reconstruction

The representation becomes a steering mechanism rather than merely a storage format.

